  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R9 reports SPS of mechanically milled CoCrFeNiTi at exactly 1000 °C, while R17 uses SPS at 1050 °C on a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-Ti alloy and obtains a fine phase distribution and high hardness. R2, R7, and R14 establish that SPS of mechanically alloyed BCC high-entropy powders can produce dense materials containing uniformly distributed oxides or other precipitates, with temperature-controlled diffusion and passivation behavior. R7 specifically connects increased SPS temperature, oxide-bearing microstructure, enhanced diffusion, and improved electrochemical passivation. Independently, R1, R3, R4, R11, and R16 show in refractory high-entropy alloys that dense, uniform oxide/passive films suppress oxygen diffusion and ionic penetration and improve oxidation and chloride-corrosion resistance. Thus an expert could reasonably propose SPS near 1000 °C to densify the MgAlTiVFeCo powder and promote protective oxide phases and passivation. The precise AlV2O4, FeAl2O4, and FeTiO3 phase identities, numerical optimum, and biphasic parabolic oxidation kinetics are not directly disclosed, but they are refinements "
